@@ -33,6 +33,8 @@ Below 768px, the page uses 20px horizontal insets to align with the conversation
 
 Select **Plugins** in the sidebar. The information button uses an 11px caption-colored icon in a 20px square hover area, with a 4px gap after the subtitle. It explains that this page configures official plugins and installs or manages other plugins, while **Settings → Built-in plugins** shows the built-in inventory and runtime status. Hover or focus shows the shared tooltip; clicking keeps it open until another click, Escape, Tab, or an outside click. The page reads the inventory and the bundles through `api-remotes` when first opened; a Host without a managed profile shows the page as unavailable. **Built-in plugin settings** lists official configuration pages by their configured order: Subagent, web search and fetch, agent loop, then shell. **Experimental plugins** shows the primary optional bundles, with **More** beside its heading opening a separate page of the same title. That page lists the official bundles eligible for display, including those on the main list, with Codex and Claude Code subagents last. Its back button returns to the main list, where **Installed plugins** remains below Experimental plugins. Bundle details return to the list they were opened from. Catalog entries keep their position after installation or removal. **Installed plugins** lists other profile bundles. Installation-carried optional bundles have no uninstall action. Experimental packages retain the **Experimental** marker on both lists. Switching on a bundle that page holds back adds it to the main list in the position that page shows it, and switching it off leaves it on that page alone unless its manifest hides unselected bundles; other bundles keep their place on both. A dependency without a bundle patch is not a plugin and is not listed unless the profile selects it, in which case it carries a problem tag. Global configuration remains in the Settings **Built-in plugins** section.
 
+**Included app plugin settings** lists non-official bundles supplied by the application, outside the profile's installed dependencies and selected bundles, when a declared row is active and the browser half registers `plugins.bundle.config` under the package name. Their configuration opens on the bundle's own page. These entries have no profile bundle switch; application ownership does not grant profile installation, update, removal, or row-control rights. The group follows inventory and slot-ledger changes without reloading and omits installation dependencies without both an active row and a registered bundle form.
+
 As available header width decreases, Refresh and Add plugin first become icon-only controls with accessible names and tooltips; the tabs then shrink from their usual minimum width to fit their labels, and the action group wraps only when necessary. The information button follows the subtitle text. The main page header and list share one scroll container. Header controls move with the scroll position until they reach their compact positions; scrolling back follows the same coordinates. The header wrapper retains its resting flow height to preserve the list's scroll range, while the draggable head's box matches its painted height so native drag geometry cannot extend into the list. A barely scrolling list can stop before the header is fully compact. The More list's back-and-title row stays fixed, and detail pages scroll their head row with the content. The Installed/Discover tabs use the compact 34px control with a 2px inset around the selection indicator. The subtitle scrolls out of view; with tabs present, the title also scrolls out, and the tabs settle beside Refresh and Add plugin where space permits. On narrow pages, the tabs and icon actions share a row beneath the heading where they fit; otherwise the tabs stay above the actions. Their relative order stays the same throughout scrolling. The resting and compact layouts retain their 28px and 16px page-top insets respectively, with 16px below the compact controls. macOS adds the frame's titlebar clearance while resting; its compact bar keeps 16px above and below with the sidebar expanded, and with the sidebar collapsed it also clears the frame's top strip so the controls stay below the native window controls. Only on macOS, when the sidebar is collapsed, the compact controls keep 8px rather than 16px above them in addition to the unchanged frame clearance, at every width. The resting layout and compact bottom spacing stay unchanged. Windows relies on the frame's caption offset without adding it again. The divider spans the page at the bottom of the compact header; the list's initial top spacing scrolls with its content. Hover styling applies only where the pointer can hover, so a tap leaves no card, row, or menu option in its hover state; cards and icon actions answer a touch-down with the pressed fill instead. The install, registry, and filter fields take a 16px face under a coarse pointer, which keeps iOS Safari from zooming a focused field.
 
 Installed bundles and their plugin rows display their own title and description in the current UI language on cards and detail pages. Each field falls back from exported locale `meta` to the accessible `package.json` of a package-root plugin; subpath plugins never read one; the final title is the full package or module name, with no package description when both sources omit it. See [Plugin display metadata](../../../docs/cookbook/adding-a-package.md#plugin-display-metadata) for the author format. Bundle cards, details, and component rows display the package root's manifest `icon` or exported `./icon`, or a subpath plugin's exported `<subpath>/icon`; absent or undecodable images retain the default artwork. Installation previews still use registry or manifest information.
